<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/lanai-interior.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/lanai-interior-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/lanai-interior.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="801" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.1"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Questions by topic</span></nav><p class="tag">Answers</p><h1>Insurance Appraisal Questions, Grouped by Topic</h1><p class="lede">Plain answers about the appraisal process, costs, deadlines and the questions that are unique to island policies.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn sun" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn line-light" href="/contact-hawaii-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div></div></section><div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><nav class="jump" aria-label="FAQ topics"><a href="#t-0">Appraisal basics</a><a href="#t-1">Costs and fees</a><a href="#t-2">Timing and deadlines</a><a href="#t-3">Hawaii-specific questions</a><a href="#t-4">Working with us</a></nav><h2 id="t-0">Appraisal basics</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>What is insurance appraisal?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>A process written into most property policies for resolving disagreements over the amount of a covered loss. Each side names an appraiser, the appraisers choose an umpire, and an award signed by any two of the three sets the amount.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is an insurance appraiser the same as a real estate appraiser?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>No. A real estate appraiser estimates market value for a sale or refinance. An insurance appraiser values the cost to repair or replace damaged property under the terms of your policy.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can appraisal decide whether my claim is covered?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Generally no. Appraisal decides the amount of loss; coverage questions, denials and bad faith are legal issues for a Hawaii attorney.</p></div></details><details><summary>Who can demand appraisal?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Either you or the insurer can, as long as the policy contains an appraisal clause. The demand should be in writing and reference the appraisal provision.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is the award binding?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>On the amount of loss, an award signed by two of the three panel members is generally binding. Courts can set awards aside in limited circumstances, which is a question for an attorney.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="t-1">Costs and fees</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who pays for appraisal?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Each side typically pays its own appraiser, and umpire fees are commonly split equally. Check your policy, since wording varies.</p></div></details><details><summary>How are appraiser fees structured?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Fees may be hourly, flat or a percentage, depending on the appraiser and the claim. Get the arrangement in writing before you sign, and ask how travel to a neighbor island is billed.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is appraisal worth it for a small gap?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>It depends on the size of the gap compared with the cost of the panel. Our claim gap fee estimator can help you see whether the numbers make sense before you start.</p></div></details><details><summary>Are inter-island travel costs extra?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Often, yes. Flights, rental cars and lodging for inspections or umpire visits on another island are commonly billed at cost or shared between the parties.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="t-2">Timing and deadlines</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>How long does appraisal take?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Simple residential disputes are often resolved in a few months. Large condo, hurricane or wildfire claims commonly take longer, especially when inspections span several islands.</p></div></details><details><summary>How long do I have to name my appraiser?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Many policies allow about 20 days after a demand, but windows vary. Read your policy and calendar the date the day you send or receive a demand.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does demanding appraisal stop my suit deadline?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Not necessarily. Your policy’s suit-limitation clause may keep running during appraisal, so speak with a Hawaii attorney if that deadline is near.</p></div></details><details><summary>When is recoverable depreciation paid?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Commonly after repairs are completed and documented, within any time limit the policy sets. Keep invoices and photos of the finished work.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="t-3">Hawaii-specific questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>What is HPIA?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>The Hawaii Property Insurance Association is the state’s FAIR plan, best known for writing basic property coverage in Big Island lava zones 1 and 2 where private carriers often decline. Recent legislative sessions have also discussed its role for condo associations, so check the current status.</p></div></details><details><summary>How does a hurricane deductible work?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Many Hawaii policies apply a separate hurricane deductible, often a percentage of dwelling coverage, when a named hurricane causes the loss. Policies vary on what triggers it, so read your declarations and endorsements.</p></div></details><details><summary>Who handles a condo claim – the AOAO or me?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>It depends on the damage. The AOAO’s master policy generally covers the building and common elements, while your HO-6 policy covers your unit’s interior, contents and possibly loss assessments; your governing documents define the line.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does lava zone matter for appraisal?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Lava zone affects which insurers will write coverage and what perils a policy covers, not how appraisal works. If a lava-related loss is covered, appraisal can still resolve a dispute over the amount.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can I file a complaint with the Insurance Division?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Yes. The Insurance Division of the Department of Commerce and Consumer Affairs accepts consumer inquiries and complaints about insurers, separately from any appraisal.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="t-4">Working with us</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Which islands do you serve?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>We work throughout Hawaii, including Oahu, Maui, the Big Island, Kauai, Molokai and Lanai. Neighbor-island inspections are scheduled around inter-island travel.</p></div></details><details><summary>Are you a law firm or a public adjuster?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Neither. We are independent appraisers focused on valuation, and we do not give legal advice or negotiate coverage.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can you review my insurer’s estimate before I demand appraisal?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Yes. An estimate review can show where the gaps are and whether appraisal looks worthwhile.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do you serve as an umpire?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Yes, when both sides and their appraisers agree on us, or when appointed. As an umpire we are neutral and have no role advocating for either party.</p></div></details><details><summary>How do I get started?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Call or email with your policy, the insurer’s estimate and any contractor bids. We will tell you plainly whether appraisal appears to fit your situation.</p></div></details></div></article><aside class="aside"><div class="box box-call"><h2 class="box-t">Talk it through first</h2><p>The first conversation is free. We’ll tell you plainly whether appraisal fits your claim.</p><a class="big" href="tel:+18445371427">+1 (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn sun" href="/contact-hawaii-appraiser/">Send claim details</a><small>Mon–Fri 7am–5pm HST</small></div><div class="box"><h2 class="box-t">Resources</h2><ul class="box-list"><li><a href="/appraisal-process-map/">Appraisal process map</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-questions-by-topic/" aria-current="page">Questions by topic</a></li><li><a href="/property-claim-glossary/">Claim glossary</a></li><li><a href="/hawaii-claim-guides/">Hawaii claim guides</a></li></ul></div></aside></div><section class="band"><div class="wrap band-in rv"><div><h2>Didn’t see your question?</h2><p>Call or email — we’re happy to talk it through.</p></div><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn sun" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn line-light" href="/contact-hawaii-appraiser/">Email the estimate</a></div></div></section>
</main>
